{
 "id": "2026-onohara-streetscape",
 "name": "Streetscape Affect in Sapporo: Place Ratings and Emotions",
 "description": "Method replication: participants see street photos of 43 places in Sapporo one at a time and answer, for each, four 7-point place statements (comfortable, safe and reassuring, open, stimulating), eight Plutchik emotions on a 3-point scale (not at all / a little / strongly), and whether they would like to go there; the images are not the paper's stimuli (Google Street View, 2024–2025, taken at the exact 43 coordinates in the authors' stimulus file). Questions are in Japanese with English glosses.\n\nOriginal study: Onohara (2026), \"Mapping Place, or Mapping Pictures? A Mapillary Pilot of Urban Streetscape Affect\", data and code on OSF (CC BY 4.0), manuscript submitted to Cities. 54 Japanese adults recruited online rated 43 Mapillary street-level images from Sapporo (residential, commercial, road, industrial and park areas; two with snow) in a Google Form, all images in a fixed order, with an optional comment per image (2,322 trial-level observations).\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) Google Street View images at the same coordinates instead of the Mapillary photos, which are not redistributed (no snow scenes); (2) images appear in random order; (3) the 7-point end labels are not in the released data and are written here as \"strongly disagree / strongly agree\"; (4) one optional comment at the end instead of one per image; (5) English glosses added to the Japanese items. Image sap_01 to sap_43 are taken at the authors' places sap_pilot_001 to sap_pilot_043, so new responses can be compared with the released trial-level data image by image.",
